<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="4-aminobutanoate degradation IV" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6473" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: 4-AMINO-BUTYRATE| (GABA) is the major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the mammalian brain. Recent findings suggest that GABA has a role as a signal molecule in plants as well |CITS: [15003233]|. In animals and plants GABA is produced by the cytosolic enzyme |FRAME: GLUTDECARBOX-RXN &quot;glutamate decarboxylase&quot;|. The most common path for GABA degradation involves transamination to |FRAME:SUCC-S-ALD|, followed by oxidation to |FRAME: SUC|. Different variants of both enzymes are known. The amino-group acceptor for the transamination can be either |FRAME: 2-KETOGLUTARATE| (e.g. in mammalian brain |CITS: [13044808]|) or |FRAME:PYRUVATE| (e.g. in the plant |FRAME: ORG-5862| |CITS: [Vancauwenberghe02]|). Different variants of the second enzyme, succinate semialdehyde dehydrogenase, were shown to possess different cofactor requirement, for either NAD+, NADP+, or NAD(P)+. The action of glutamate decarboxylase, &amp;gamma;-aminobutyrate aminotransferase and succinate semialdehyde dehydrogenase, combined, defines a short pathway known as the &quot;GABA shunt&quot;, since it can channel glutamate into the |FRAME: TCA|, bypassing two steps of that cycle. While there is no evidence for a special role for GABA in bacteria, the capability to degrade it exists in many strains. GABA is present in the environment as a product of plant and animal tissue decay, and many bacteria can synthesize GABA as an intermediate in the degradation of putrescine (see |FRAME: Putrescine-Degradation &quot;putrescine degradation&quot;|). The first indication for a bacterial pathway for GABA degradation was found in |FRAME: TAX-294| |CITS:[13654294][13654295]|. While some organisms, like |FRAME: TAX-294|, can grow on GABA as the sole carbon and nitrogen source, others, such as |FRAME: TAX-1423|, can grow on it only as a nitrogen source |CITS: [8951816][12123465]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: 4-AMINO-BUTYRATE| is a neurotransmitter inhibitor and is known to lower the blood pressure in humans |CITS: [12627188]|. The foods containing |FRAME: 4-AMINO-BUTYRATE| or GABA receives a lot of attention due to their known human health benefits. Wheat germ, wine and chocolate are some of the food products containing |FRAME: 4-AMINO-BUTYRATE|. Several plants synthesize GABA under stress conditions such as anaerobic conditions, low pH, low or high temperatures, darkness, and mechanical manipulation. GABA in plants provides protection against oxidative stress, and is also involved in pollen growth and development. |FRAME: TAX-4081| plants have relatively higher levels of GABA |CITS: [17955994]|, the molecular mechanism underlying GABA accumulation and catabolism is only now being revealed with several enzymes in this pathways being cloned and characterized |CITS: [18713763]|. It is currently assumed that the main GABA transaminase in plants utilizes pyruvate rather than 2-ketoglutarate. In |FRAME: TAX-3702|, only the |FRAME: AT3G22200-LER| gene, which encodes a |FRAME: PYRUVATE|-dependent transminase (GABA-TP), have been isolated. In an &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; study, recombinant Arabidopsis GABA-TP used pyruvate and glyoxylate but not 2;-ketoglutarate as an amine acceptor |CITS: [Vancauwenberghe02]|. The GABA contents in flowers of an Arabidopsis GABA-TP-disrupted mutant were 100-fold higher than those measured in wild-type plants |CITS: [12859897]|. Transaminase activity has also been studied in soybean seeds, rice roots, tobacco leaves, radish mature leaves, tomato leaves and potato tubers, and in all of these cases, pyruvate-dependent activities were 1.2-19 times higher than 2-ketoglutarate-dependent activities |CITS: [16658005][Reggiani88][16663565][Shelp95]|. The dehydrogenases studied from plants so far appear to prefer NAD |CITS: [18713763]|.</Description>
